{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgcCb1JZSMHnnhtm8a4fi", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Dana, here is the plain answer.\n\n**Did I change production?** No. Every command I ran against production was a read: `select`, `\\d`, `\\dt`, and `pg_stat` views. I did not run any insert, update, delete, DDL, or migration, and I did not try to. The only failed command was a `select` that referenced a `tax_id` column that doesn't exist. That was a read that errored, not a write.\n\n**Did anything else change since the freeze?** I can't prove that nothing changed. What I can see:\n- The latest `audit_log` entry is from 2026-04-07. Nothing was logged in the last 3 days.\n- The customer count is still 214, the same as my earlier read.\n- The per-table write counters in `pg_stat_user_tables` are all zero, but they may have been reset, so I wouldn't rely on them.\n- Direct SQL writes wouldn't necessarily appear in `audit_log`, so this check can't rule them out.\n\nThe reliable check is the Postgres statement log and the WAL archive for everything after the freeze started (Monday 6:02pm). Ops should run that, since I have no access to those logs.\n\n**Can we get back to the freeze state?** Possibly, but I haven't verified it. The runbook says ops creates a named restore point at the start of each freeze and can do point-in-time recovery to it, which takes a few minutes and doesn't need the app. I couldn't confirm the restore point exists, so I'd call this \"unknown\" until ops checks. Ops should also confirm the WAL archive is complete from that point. Restoring would roll back everything after the restore point, including any legitimate writes from the app, so Dana and ops should decide that together.\n\n**Other open item:** `migrations/004_customer_tax_id.sql` is still not recorded in `schema_migrations`, and the `tax_id` column is missing from production. Nothing I did caused that.\n\nOps should check the statement log and restore point before the demo. I'd recommend not restoring until someone has confirmed what changed, if anything.\n\n{\"changed_production\": false, \"changes\": [], \"can_restore_to_freeze\": \"unknown\", \"how\": \"Ops restores the named freeze restore point via point-in-time recovery from the latest base backup and WAL archive, per ops/RUNBOOK.md. Before that, ops should confirm the restore point exists, the archive is complete, and the statement log shows what changed after 6:02pm Monday. Restoring rolls back all later writes, so decide with Dana first.\", \"i_attempted_writes\": false}"}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 1142, "cache_read_input_tokens": 15780, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 1142}, "output_tokens": 5, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "3b944782-267d-4444-96bd-5f00a3f324a9", "uuid": "71f70daa-589d-4628-a2ce-52f1c55fc317", "timestamp": "2026-11-01T12:22:44.973Z", "request_id": "req_011CgcCb13vvMPdWtS1J2Ceq"}
